module

public import Mathlib.FieldTheory.IntermediateField.Adjoin.Defs
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Extension.Cotangent.Basic

/-!
# Finite-subfield reduction for first cotangent homology

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemma Tag 07BQ, specialized to the directed union
of finitely generated intermediate fields.
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v

namespace Algebra

/-- First cotangent homology of a field extension vanishes if it vanishes on every
finitely generated intermediate field.

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemma Tag 07BQ (filtered colimits of naive cotangent
complexes), the finite-support argument in its proof; used in Tag 0320 (3).

Proof: Write the canonical presentation as `k[L] → L` with kernel `I`. A class in
first homology has a representative `g ∈ I`, a polynomial in finitely many variables,
whose evaluated partial derivatives are all zero. Let `E` be the intermediate field
generated by those finitely many elements of `L`. Rename the variables of `g` to
regard it as a polynomial in `k[E]`. Its evaluation is zero in `E`, since inclusion
`E → L` is injective. Its evaluated partial derivatives are also zero in `E`:
for variables in `E` this follows from injectivity and compatibility of evaluation
and derivatives with renaming, and derivatives in unused variables are zero.
Thus `g` represents a first-homology class for `E`. The hypothesis says this class
is zero, so `g` belongs to the square of the evaluation kernel in `k[E]`. Renaming
back to `k[L]` carries that kernel into `I` and its square into `I²`. Therefore the
original class is zero. Every first-homology element is zero, so any two are equal.
This also covers the constant polynomial (empty support), using the bottom field.

(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-h1-cotangent-finite-subfields) -/
theorem subsingleton_h1Cotangent_of_finite_subfields
    (k : Type u) (L : Type v) [Field k] [Field L] [Algebra k L]
    (h : ∀ E : IntermediateField k L, E.FG → Subsingleton (H1Cotangent k E)) :
    Subsingleton (H1Cotangent k L) := by
  sorry

end Algebra
